{"summary":"Accept all three. The registry recomputation concern is real, though the safe fix is to share one registry snapshot through the breakdown rather than assume the cached registry is always current. The denitrification test comment is backwards. The ROL change also altered the base substrate-demand model by deleting rooted-plant O2 demand, which conflates baseline demand with the new bonus parameter.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-6-5c9d4bed","decision":"accept","rationale":"`root_oxygenation_active_biomass_g()` rebuilds the full habitat registry inside the substrate hot path, while the same breakdown already uses `state.habitat_registry` for substrate area fraction. That is redundant work and it also mixes registry sources inside one calculation. The amendment is directionally correct, but the robust fix is to compute or pass one registry snapshot through the whole breakdown; using `state.habitat_registry` unconditionally is not always safe because the hourly engine refresh happens after `step_substrate_zones`."},{"amendment_id":"fr-6-8b38a232","decision":"accept","rationale":"The comment’s causal explanation is incorrect. A deeper oxic zone leaves less suboxic volume, so hourly denitrification capacity should decrease, which is exactly what the later test asserts. Since this integration test only checks for divergence, the wrong prose is misleading maintenance guidance and should be corrected."},{"amendment_id":"fr-6-e14eeefd","decision":"accept","rationale":"`estimate_substrate_o2_demand_rate()` no longer includes any rooted-biomass demand, so `rol_rate_cm_per_g = 0` now makes a planted bed behave like an unplanted bed in the base penetration model. That means the new ROL parameter no longer represents only added root oxygen supply; it also implicitly removes the prior rooted-demand effect. Folding those opposing mechanisms into one knob changes baseline semantics and makes calibration and reasoning less inspectable."}]}